This page contains no derivations, formulas or computations. It is a planning and administrative page in Hilbert's notebook, probably from his Königsberg period (c. 1890–1893, judging by the Poincaré references and the neighbouring pages listing doctoral topics). It has three parts.

(a) A struck-out reading list, cancelled by a header line, a footer line and many vertical strokes: "Die Schriften, welche ich zu studieren beabsichtige: 1) Poincaré, Ueber das 3 Körperproblem (Preisschrift, Acta); Poincaré (Fuchsische Gruppen u. Functionen, Acta I.)". The first item is Poincaré's King Oscar prize memoir "Sur le problème des trois corps et les équations de la dynamique", Acta Math. 13 (1890). The second is his memoirs on Fuchsian groups and functions, Acta Math. 1 (1882). This fits the previous page, where Hilbert speaks of proving convergence of series "wie bei Poincaré" (i.e. like Poincaré's theta-Fuchsian series) for his zeta-like functions f = sum 1/psi^s.

(b) "Thema zum Staatsexamen": "The concept of uniform convergence of infinite series and its significance for function theory." This is an examination essay topic (Staatsexamen = state teacher's examination) that Hilbert set or planned. Its mathematical content is standard Weierstrassian analysis:
- the definition of uniform convergence;
- continuity of the uniform limit;
- termwise integration and differentiation;
- the Weierstrass M-test;
- Weierstrass's theorem that a locally uniform limit of holomorphic functions is holomorphic.

(c) A pasted slip: "Thema für Siedentopf: Darstellung der Theorie der Fourierschen Reihen mit besonderer Berücksichtigung der verschiedenen Criterien für die Entwickelbarkeit einer Function einer reellen Veränderlichen in eine Fourier-Reihe." This is a topic assigned to a student named Siedentopf: an exposition of Fourier series with special attention to the various criteria for the expandability of a function of a real variable. Such criteria include those of Dirichlet, Riemann's localization principle, Lipschitz, Dini, Jordan (bounded variation, 1881) and du Bois-Reymond's counterexample (1873).

Nothing is computed; the page documents Hilbert's reading plans and his supervision or examination duties.
